# Welcome & Developer Roadmap

Start here to understand how our mobile engineering documentation fits together and what to complete before contributing to an application.

This onboarding section supports **Android, iOS, and React Native engineers**, including developers moving from web or backend development. Workstation installation instructions cover **macOS on Apple Silicon only**.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep this documentation and company source material within approved company systems. Never include passwords, access tokens, signing material, personal data, or unredacted sensitive logs in examples, screenshots, support requests, or AI prompts. Use only company-approved tools and sharing destinations.

## 1. What you will achieve

By the end of onboarding, you should be able to:

- Explain the purpose of your application and identify its owning team.
- Access the repositories and development services required for your assigned work.
- Configure a compatible workstation using the project's approved toolchain.
- Build and run the assigned application or approved starter locally.
- Find the architecture, platform, testing, and security guidance relevant to a change.
- Submit a focused first pull request with meaningful verification evidence.

Onboarding completion is based on these outcomes. Access approvals, project requirements, and build dependencies can affect the time needed.

## 2. Choose your reading path

Both paths use the same setup and contribution guides. Choose the path that matches your experience; revisit the mobile foundations guide whenever a concept is unfamiliar.

| Your background | Recommended reading order |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Experienced mobile engineer** | This page → Platform Overview → Machine Setup → First Local Run → First Contribution |
| **Web, backend, or other engineer new to mobile** | This page → Platform Overview → Mobile Foundations → Machine Setup → First Local Run → First Contribution |

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    START["Start: Welcome and Roadmap"] --> OVERVIEW["Understand the Mobile Platform"]
    OVERVIEW --> EXPERIENCE{"New to Mobile Development?"}
    EXPERIENCE -->|Yes| FOUNDATIONS["Learn Mobile Foundations"]
    EXPERIENCE -->|No| SETUP["Prepare Access and Workstation"]
    FOUNDATIONS --> SETUP
    SETUP --> RUN["Build and Run the Assigned App"]
    RUN --> CHANGE["Complete a First Contribution"]
    CHANGE --> READY["Continue with the Relevant Engineering Guides"]
```

## 3. Follow the onboarding pages in order

| Step | Page | What you will do | Completion evidence |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **01** | **[Platform Overview](/mobile/01-onboarding/platform-overview/)** | Understand the ecosystem, repository responsibilities, supported stacks, and how to approach platform selection. | You can identify your application, stack, owning team, and relevant repositories. |
| **02** | **[Mobile Foundations](/mobile/01-onboarding/non-mobile-engineer-guide/)** | Learn mobile lifecycle, UI state, networking, local storage, native capabilities, and distribution concepts. Recommended for engineers new to mobile. | You can explain the main differences that affect your first mobile change. |
| **03** | **[Machine Setup — macOS Apple Silicon](/mobile/01-onboarding/machine-setup-guide/)** | Request necessary access, install the required tools, and verify your workstation. | Required access works and the checks for your assigned platform pass. |
| **04** | **[First Local Run](/mobile/01-onboarding/first-local-run/)** | Obtain the assigned application or approved starter, use its locked dependencies, configure local settings, and build and run it. | The expected app screen opens on the documented simulator, emulator, or approved device. |
| **05** | **[First Contribution](/mobile/01-onboarding/new-engineer-onboarding/)** | Make a team-agreed change, add or update tests, run required checks, and submit it for review. | Your PR includes the change, verification results, and review information required by the repository. |

**All workstation installation instructions live in Machine Setup.** Later guides reference that page rather than repeat installation commands.

## 4. Confirm your starting point

Before installing tools or creating a repository, agree on the following with your team:

| Confirm | Why it matters |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Assigned application or approved starter** | An existing application and a newly generated application have different starting steps. |
| **Platform and target devices** | Install the tools needed for your work. React Native applications still require native tooling for each target platform. |
| **Repository, source branch, and setup instructions** | Start from the intended source and follow the application's documented workflow. |
| **Supported toolchain and dependency versions** | Use the versions required by the project and approved template. Installing the newest version of every tool can introduce incompatibilities. |
| **Access requirements and approvers** | Request the permissions needed for your responsibilities. Store administration and production signing access are not universal onboarding prerequisites. |
| **First task and reviewer** | A small, agreed change gives you a concrete way to validate your development workflow. |

For an existing application, follow its established stack and architecture. A new application's stack selection should be reviewed with the responsible team using the Platform Overview guidance.

## 5. Know where each topic belongs

Use this map as your work moves beyond the first local build.

| Documentation section | Open it when you need to… |
| :--- | :--- |
| **1. Onboarding** | Set up your environment, understand mobile basics, and start contributing. |
| **2. Architecture** | Understand dependency boundaries, data flow, resilience, feature flags, and branching and PR conventions. |
| **3. Platforms & SDKs** | Implement Android, iOS, or React Native features and integrate shared app foundations. React Native guidance uses Redux Toolkit. |
| **4. GenAI & Developer Tools** | Use approved development tools, rule files, and AI-assisted workflows with controlled context and human verification. |
| **5. Testing & Quality** | Write meaningful tests, apply coverage requirements, and validate behavior locally and on Sauce Labs. |
| **6. Observability** | Integrate Datadog, protect telemetry data, and investigate application performance and failures. |
| **7. CI/CD** | Enroll an application and understand governed validation, builds, testing, signing, delivery, and recovery. |
| **8. App Store & Distribution** | Prepare distribution destinations, testing tracks, submissions, releases, and availability verification. |
| **9. Support & FAQ** | Find help and answers to recurring questions. |

Testing and observability are part of feature development. Consult their guidance while implementing a change, before preparing it for release.

## 6. Separate local development from enrollment and release

A successful local build confirms that you can build and run the application in the environment you tested. It does not confirm CI access, distribution signing, store approval, or availability to users.

For a new application, the CI/CD section describes the governed enrollment process. Developers submit a secret-free configuration request; approved platform automation provisions the external resources. **Do not manually create a Bitrise app or upload production credentials as an onboarding shortcut.**

For an existing application, use its established setup. Do not submit a new enrollment request simply to obtain a local build or investigate a pipeline failure.

Release completion requires destination-specific evidence. A passed build alone does not establish that a signed application is distributed to its intended audience.

## 7. Work safely and ask for help effectively

- Follow the Machine Setup guide for corporate access, network, and trust configuration. Do not disable certificate validation to bypass connection failures.
- Obtain local configuration through the team's approved process. Keep real secrets out of tracked files and command output shared with others.
- Treat AI-generated code and tests as drafts. Review them against the repository's rules and execute the relevant checks before submission.
- When blocked, report the step, platform, source revision, expected result, actual result, and a sanitized error excerpt through the approved support channel.
- For signing, provisioning, or distribution failures, preserve the existing state and follow the relevant recovery guide. Avoid unverified changes to credentials or external resources.

## 8. Onboarding completion checklist

Use this checklist with your team. Record evidence in the team's approved location without copying sensitive values into it.

- [ ] I understand the application, owning team, and repositories relevant to my role.
- [ ] I have completed Mobile Foundations or reviewed the concepts relevant to my experience.
- [ ] My required access works and my macOS Apple Silicon setup checks pass.
- [ ] I have built and run the assigned application from the intended source revision.
- [ ] I know where to find architecture and platform implementation standards.
- [ ] I understand the testing, coverage, and telemetry requirements that apply to my change.
- [ ] I have submitted a focused first PR with the required checks and verification evidence.
- [ ] I have addressed review feedback and recorded any remaining onboarding blockers.
- [ ] I know how to request support without exposing confidential information.

**Next: [Platform Overview](/mobile/01-onboarding/platform-overview/)** — understand the platform and your project's place within it before preparing your workstation.



