# Platform Overview & Stack Selection

The mobile platform brings together application standards, shared foundations, development tools, testing services, observability, and governed delivery. Application teams use these capabilities to build and operate Android, iOS, and React Native applications.

This page explains **what each part does, where responsibilities sit, and how to approach stack selection**. Installation commands belong in [Machine Setup](/mobile/01-onboarding/machine-setup-guide/); implementation details belong in Architecture and Platforms & SDKs.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Keep source, configuration, operational evidence, and company identifiers within approved systems. Examples must not contain real credentials, private artifact links, or sensitive user data.

## 1. Identify your starting point

| Your assignment | Start here |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Contribute to an existing app** | Confirm its owning team, repository, source branch, established stack, and project instructions. Follow the existing implementation unless a change has been explicitly agreed. |
| **Create a new app** | Confirm the business requirements, supported devices, owning team, approved starter, and stack decision before generating source or requesting enrollment. |
| **Learn mobile development** | Read this overview, then complete [Mobile Foundations](/mobile/01-onboarding/non-mobile-engineer-guide/) before workstation setup. |

Do not create a new repository, replace a framework, or enroll another CI application simply to work on an existing app.

## 2. Understand the ecosystem

Two connected paths support an application: the **engineering and delivery path** prepares a release; the **runtime path** serves users after installation.

| Path | Main components | Purpose |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Engineering and delivery** | Source control, review, automated checks, Bitrise, device testing, distribution destinations | Validate committed changes, build artifacts, and deliver through approved routes. |
| **Runtime** | Installed app, shared foundations, backend APIs, approved telemetry services | Present features, manage state and data, communicate with services, and report permitted diagnostic information. |

The installed app does not invoke Bitrise to serve a user request. CI/CD operates on source and release artifacts.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant DEV as Developer
    participant GIT as Source Repository
    participant CI as Governed CI
    participant DEST as Distribution Destination
    participant APP as Installed App
    participant API as Backend Services
    participant OBS as Observability

    DEV->>GIT: Submit source and reviewed tests
    GIT->>CI: Trigger configured validation or build
    CI->>CI: Execute required checks and build
    Note over CI,DEST: Delivery requires the applicable gates and authorization
    CI->>DEST: Deliver approved artifact through configured route
    DEST-->>APP: Make eligible version available to intended users
    APP->>API: Request business data through approved integration
    API-->>APP: Return response
    APP->>OBS: Send permitted diagnostic events
    Note over APP,OBS: Consent and privacy controls govern collection
```

This is a responsibility map, not a promise that every source event triggers delivery. Exact triggers, approvals, and destinations are defined by the approved pipeline and release process.

## 3. Know which repository does what

The platform's source, configuration, provisioning engine, and workflow catalog have different responsibilities. They are not interchangeable.

| Repository or resource | Responsibility | What an application developer needs to know |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Application source repository** | Application code, dependency locks, tests, and project configuration. | This is where you implement and review feature changes. Record the source branch and commit when investigating a build. |
| **Approved application template** | Generates the starting application structure. | The template repository is a generator; its root is not the generated application. Follow the approved template's instructions. |
| **Platform configuration repository** | Declarative enrollment requests and their review history. | A new application uses a secret-free request following the current schema and catalog. |
| **Provisioning engine repository** | Validation and live-apply implementation. | Platform automation performs provisioning. Developers inspect sanitized results through the approved process. |
| **Workflow template catalog** | Approved workflow definitions and versions. | Select a version approved for the requested platform and route. Historical workshop versions are not automatic approval for new use. |
| **Shared foundation and SDK repositories** | Reusable platform capabilities and their contracts. | Use documented, compatible versions. Confirm actual package names and APIs in the relevant integration guide. |

A source commit identifies application code. A configuration merge commit identifies an enrollment or configuration change. Record both when relevant; one does not substitute for the other.

## 4. Understand the supported stacks

| Stack | Application code and UI | Key consideration |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Native Android** | Kotlin and the Android UI approach established by the project. | Direct Android platform integration, Android device requirements, and compatibility with vendor SDKs. |
| **Native iOS** | Swift and the iOS UI approach established by the project. | Direct Apple platform integration, supported iOS devices, and compatibility with required Apple capabilities. |
| **React Native** | TypeScript and React Native, with **Redux Toolkit** for Redux state management. | Shared feature implementation across Android and iOS, while retaining native projects, platform configuration, and device-specific verification. |

React Native does not remove the need for Android and iOS knowledge. Platform permissions, signing, native dependencies, lifecycle behavior, and release packaging still require platform-specific work.

Availability of a shared SDK must be checked per platform and version. A capability supported in one stack should not be assumed to have an equivalent wrapper in another.

## 5. Select a stack for a new application

Use requirements and evidence to make the decision with the responsible team. Team familiarity is an input, alongside device support, integration risk, accessibility, performance, security, and long-term maintenance.

| Question | What to evaluate |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Which operating systems and devices must we support?** | Android only, iOS only, both platforms, managed devices, and required OS versions. |
| **Which native capabilities are essential?** | Vendor SDK support, scanners, Bluetooth, background execution, camera, device management, or other platform services. |
| **Can a shared implementation meet the requirements?** | Availability and maintenance of native modules, platform-specific UI requirements, and the amount of custom native work. |
| **What performance must be demonstrated?** | Representative workloads on target hardware. Measure uncertain interactions instead of relying on framework rankings. |
| **Can the team maintain the entire application?** | Skills for feature development, native integration, testing, debugging, accessibility, and release support. |
| **Does the platform support the proposed delivery path?** | Approved starter, SDK compatibility, workflow catalog, device testing, signing, and distribution requirements. |

Typical starting points for evaluation:

- **Android-only product:** evaluate native Android against the device and integration requirements.
- **iOS-only product:** evaluate native iOS against the required Apple capabilities and UI behavior.
- **Product targeting both platforms:** evaluate React Native and separate native implementations against the same requirements and maintenance expectations.
- **High-risk native integration:** prove the uncertain capability on target hardware before committing to a stack. Hardware integration does not automatically rule out React Native.

Do not assume a fixed percentage of shared code, a universal performance winner, or a guaranteed delivery speed.

### Record the decision

Use the team's decision-record location. A concise record should include:

```markdown
# Mobile Stack Decision

- Application and owner:
- Required platforms, OS versions, and devices:
- Critical user journeys:
- Native or vendor integrations:
- Security, accessibility, and performance requirements:
- Options evaluated:
- Prototype or compatibility evidence:
- Selected stack and reasons:
- Trade-offs and unresolved risks:
- Approved starter and delivery compatibility:
- Reviewers and decision date:
```

For an existing application, consult its decision history before proposing a different stack.

## 6. Apply shared principles through platform-specific code

The stacks share architectural goals; their directories, APIs, and lifecycle mechanisms differ.

| Concern | Shared expectation | Detailed guidance |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Dependency boundaries** | Keep business rules independent of concrete UI, network, and persistence frameworks. Use contracts to separate policy from implementation. | Architecture; platform standards |
| **State and lifecycle** | Make state ownership explicit and prevent cancelled or outdated work from replacing current results. | Android, iOS, and React Native standards |
| **Data and resilience** | Define caching, freshness, account scope, timeouts, failure handling, and any retry or circuit-breaker policy. | Architecture; data integration examples |
| **Shared foundations** | Integrate supported design, localization, security, notification, branding, and cleanup capabilities through documented contracts. | Platforms & SDKs |
| **Testing** | Verify behavior with meaningful assertions and meet the defined coverage requirement. Coverage alone is not proof of correctness. | Testing & Quality |
| **Telemetry** | Collect only approved data, respect consent, and verify release-specific diagnostics. | Observability |
| **AI assistance** | Use approved tools and context boundaries; review and execute generated code and tests. | GenAI & Developer Tools |

A circuit breaker is one resilience mechanism, not a guarantee against crashes. Feature flags are configuration controls, not substitutes for server-side authorization. Detailed guidance belongs in Architecture rather than in onboarding examples.

## 7. Separate enrollment, builds, and release

| Activity | What happens | What it does not establish |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Local development** | You build and run the intended source on a documented local target. | CI readiness or distribution availability. |
| **Request validation** | Local and PR checks inspect a secret-free enrollment request and provide review evidence. | Creation of a live Bitrise app. |
| **Protected promotion and live apply** | Approved platform automation provisions resources after the governed configuration promotion. | Successful publication of every requested route. |
| **Later builds** | The enrolled application runs its configured workflows against identified source. | Successful delivery merely because compilation passed. |
| **Distribution verification** | Evidence confirms the intended artifact, signing, destination version, audience, and availability. | Success for a different route, platform, or version. |

The governed route names are `qa_android`, `qa_ios`, `uat_android`, and `uat_ios`. Applicability is determined by the approved platform template. Verify each applicable route independently; a QA result does not prove a UAT result.

Developers do not manually provision Bitrise applications or paste production signing credentials into the web console as part of onboarding. Follow CI/CD guidance for enrollment and recovery, and App Store & Distribution guidance for destination requirements.

## 8. Confirm responsibilities

These boundaries guide collaboration; confirm the actual owners and approvers for your application.

| Responsibility | Expected collaboration |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Feature implementation** | Application developers implement changes, tests, and instrumentation with reviewers. |
| **Architecture and stack decisions** | The application team and responsible architecture or platform owners review significant choices. |
| **Governed provisioning and workflow changes** | Platform owners manage approved automation and investigate provisioning outcomes. |
| **Signing and credential access** | Authorized owners manage protected credentials and bindings through the approved process. |
| **Quality and release evidence** | Developers, QA, and release owners verify the requirements relevant to their responsibilities. |
| **Production operation** | The owning team follows agreed monitoring, support, and escalation arrangements. |

## 9. Before you continue

- [ ] I know whether I am joining an existing app or starting a new one.
- [ ] I can identify the owning team, source repository, and established or approved stack.
- [ ] I understand the difference between application source, templates, configuration, and provisioning automation.
- [ ] I know where implementation, testing, telemetry, and delivery guidance lives.
- [ ] I understand that enrollment, a successful build, and verified distribution are separate outcomes.

**New to mobile?** Continue to [Mobile Foundations](/mobile/01-onboarding/non-mobile-engineer-guide/).

**Already experienced in mobile?** Continue to [Machine Setup — macOS Apple Silicon](/mobile/01-onboarding/machine-setup-guide/).





