# Mobile Foundations for Web & Backend Engineers

Your experience with components, APIs, domain logic, and automated tests transfers to mobile development. The additional challenge is managing software installed on a user's device: connectivity changes, the operating system controls execution, and users may remain on older versions.

This page introduces the concepts needed before [Machine Setup](/mobile/01-onboarding/machine-setup-guide/). Experienced mobile engineers can use it as a refresher. Full implementations belong in Architecture and Platforms & SDKs.

> **Confidential — internal use only.** Use synthetic data in learning exercises. Keep credentials, sensitive telemetry, private artifacts, and company source within approved systems.

## 1. Start with the right mental model

The app is a client of backend services. It manages presentation, interaction, and permitted local data; the backend remains responsible for authorization and authoritative server-side business decisions.

| Familiar concept | Mobile consideration | Practical consequence |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Updating a deployed service** | Installed app versions can coexist for an extended period. | Design API compatibility and migration behavior explicitly. A source merge does not update every user's device. |
| **An active browser page or server process** | Apps and screens have lifecycles controlled partly by the OS. | Scope work to its owner and recover from interruption. |
| **Network requests** | A device can have connectivity without being able to reach the API. | Handle timeouts, expired sessions, server failures, and unavailable networks separately. |
| **Application state** | Screen state, shared session state, and persisted data have different lifetimes. | Choose ownership and storage deliberately. |
| **Server configuration and credentials** | Client binaries and client-accessible configuration can be inspected. | Keep privileged secrets on protected services. Remote configuration is not a secret vault. |
| **Automated checks** | Device behavior adds permissions, rotation, backgrounding, and OS differences. | Combine logic tests with integration, UI, and device tests. |

These concerns also appear in web and backend systems. Mobile makes device lifecycle and installed-version compatibility central to everyday development.

## 2. Understand lifecycle and cancellation

A screen can disappear while a request is running. The app can move to the background, lose its process, or restart with a new session. Background execution is restricted and differs by platform and task type.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor USER as User
    participant SCREEN as Screen
    participant STATE as State Owner
    participant DATA as Data Layer

    USER->>SCREEN: Open feature
    SCREEN->>STATE: Request data for current account
    STATE->>DATA: Start asynchronous operation
    USER->>SCREEN: Leave feature or change account
    SCREEN->>STATE: End ownership or invalidate request
    STATE->>DATA: Cancel work where supported
    DATA-->>STATE: Operation completes or is cancelled
    Note over STATE: Reject results that no longer belong to current state
```

Cancellation is cooperative: it may not stop a request already processed by a server. It also does not replace protection against stale results.

| Event | Required consideration |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Navigation away** | Cancel screen-owned work when appropriate; shared work may have a longer-lived owner. |
| **Rotation or UI recreation** | Preserve suitable state through the platform's state owner rather than restarting every operation blindly. |
| **Backgrounding** | Save necessary progress using supported mechanisms. Do not assume an arbitrary task will keep running. |
| **Process termination** | Reconstruct the app from persisted, permitted state. Cleanup callbacks are not guaranteed to run. |
| **Logout or account switch** | Invalidate prior requests and prevent one account's data from appearing in another account's UI or cache. |

Use the platform standards for lifecycle-bound coroutines, Swift concurrency, or React Native effect and async-request ownership.

## 3. Separate UI state, business rules, and data access

A useful way to reason about a feature is:

1. The screen renders state and emits user actions.
2. A state owner coordinates work and exposes the next UI state.
3. Business rules decide what the operation means.
4. Repository contracts hide concrete network and persistence implementations from those rules.

The Architecture section defines dependency direction. This list describes runtime responsibilities, not a universal folder structure.

| Kind of state | Example | Usual owner |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Screen-local state** | Whether a disclosure panel is expanded. | Screen or component. |
| **Feature state** | Loaded items, refresh progress, or a recoverable failure. | Feature ViewModel or the established feature state mechanism. |
| **Shared application state** | Current session or app-wide preferences. | Established application-level owner. React Native uses Redux Toolkit where Redux state is needed. |
| **Persisted data** | Account-scoped cached records or allowed preferences. | Repository or dedicated storage abstraction. |

Do not move every temporary UI value into a global store. Do not let a screen directly assemble authentication headers, SQL queries, and business decisions.

### Small example: represent a feature outcome explicitly

The following snippets illustrate **data models only**, not complete screens or SDK integrations. Each represents the same read-only feature with loading, empty, content, and error states.

**Android — Kotlin**

```kotlin
data class BalanceItem(val id: String, val label: String)

sealed interface BalanceUiState {
    data object Loading : BalanceUiState
    data object Empty : BalanceUiState
    data class Content(val items: List<BalanceItem>) : BalanceUiState
    data class Error(val canRetry: Boolean) : BalanceUiState
}
```

**iOS — Swift**

```swift
struct BalanceItem: Identifiable, Equatable, Sendable {
    let id: String
    let label: String
}

enum BalanceUIState: Equatable, Sendable {
    case loading
    case empty
    case content([BalanceItem])
    case error(canRetry: Bool)
}
```

**React Native — TypeScript**

```typescript
type BalanceItem = Readonly<{ id: string; label: string }>;

type BalanceUiState =
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'empty' }
  | { status: 'content'; items: readonly BalanceItem[] }
  | { status: 'error'; canRetry: boolean };
```

**Hint:** Convert raw failures into safe, localized presentation messages. Do not render a server response body or exception string directly to the user. A feature that keeps content visible during refresh will need a richer state model than this introductory example.

## 4. Design for interrupted networks

A connection indicator is not proof that a request will succeed. Attempt the operation and handle its actual result.

| Situation | Expected design |
| :--- | :--- |
| **No usable network or timeout** | Show a recoverable state. Offer retry where safe; show permitted cached content when the feature supports it. |
| **Expired authentication** | Follow the approved session renewal or sign-in flow. Avoid independent refresh loops in every screen. |
| **Permission denied by the server** | Respect the denial. A hidden button or enabled feature flag does not grant authorization. |
| **Backend failure** | Apply the agreed error and resilience policy. Do not retry indefinitely. |
| **Unknown result after a write** | Establish whether the write occurred before resubmitting it. Use the backend's supported idempotency or reconciliation contract. |
| **Stale cached content** | Make freshness and refresh behavior clear when it affects user decisions. |

A circuit breaker can reduce repeated calls to a failing dependency. It does not provide offline storage, authorize a retry, or prevent all crashes.

Offline support is a product decision. Some features allow cached reads; others require a live response. Offline writes require an explicit design for queues, duplicates, conflicts, expiry, and account scope.

## 5. Store only what the feature needs

| Data | Guidance |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Temporary UI state** | Keep in memory unless restoration is required. |
| **Non-sensitive preferences** | Use the project's preference abstraction and documented defaults. |
| **Authentication material** | Use the approved secure-storage and authentication integration. Do not place tokens in ordinary preferences, logs, or Redux state persisted without appropriate controls. |
| **Business records** | Define permission to store, account scope, freshness, retention, and deletion behavior. Use encryption where required by the data classification. |
| **Diagnostics** | Collect only approved attributes. Avoid raw payloads and identifiers that disclose sensitive information. |

Logout is more than changing the visible screen. It must coordinate session invalidation, in-flight work, account-scoped caches, and other resources defined by the application. Follow the shared cleanup design; do not promise that a simple file deletion guarantees forensic erasure.

## 6. Keep the UI responsive and accessible

Network access, database operations, and expensive computation must use the appropriate asynchronous or background mechanisms. Declaring a function asynchronous does not automatically move CPU-heavy work off the UI thread or the React Native JavaScript thread.

For every user journey:

- Provide meaningful loading, empty, content, and error states.
- Prevent accidental duplicate submissions while preserving accessible feedback.
- Use semantic controls, accessible labels, and stable automation identifiers.
- Support the text sizing, localization, and input behavior required by the app.
- Request permissions when needed and handle refusal without trapping the user.
- Validate on the target platform; simulator or emulator success does not prove hardware behavior.

Automation identifiers help tests find a control. They do not replace accessible labels that explain it to a person.

## 7. Understand native integration in React Native

React Native shares JavaScript or TypeScript feature code, but produces Android and iOS applications with native dependencies and configuration.

| Concern | What still needs platform-specific attention |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Permissions** | Android manifest and runtime permissions; iOS capability and usage-description requirements. |
| **Native SDKs** | Compatible Android and iOS implementations, supported native-module integration, and release configuration. |
| **Notifications and links** | Platform registration, routing, lifecycle handling, and authorization of the destination. |
| **Builds** | Native toolchains, dependency locks, application identifiers, and signing. |
| **Testing** | Android and iOS behavior, accessibility locators, OS differences, and real-device capabilities. |

Use Redux Toolkit according to the React Native standards. Native modules, network clients, and business rules should not be scattered across screen components.

## 8. Distinguish build artifacts and distribution

| Term | Meaning |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Android APK** | An installable Android package, subject to device compatibility, signing, and installation policy. |
| **Android AAB** | An Android App Bundle used for publishing. It is not installed directly like an APK; distribution tooling produces installable APKs. |
| **iOS IPA** | A packaged iOS app whose installation depends on the applicable signing, provisioning, and distribution method. |
| **Simulator build** | A build for a simulator environment. It is not interchangeable with a physical-device release artifact. |
| **Code signing** | Platform-specific mechanisms used to establish application integrity and signing identity. Signing alone does not establish store approval or user availability. |
| **Verified distribution** | Evidence that the intended version is available through the intended destination to the intended audience. |

A local debug build is for development. Share application builds with testers through the approved distribution process. Do not distribute production signing material or private artifact URLs as a shortcut.

The CI/CD section explains governed automation. App Store & Distribution explains destination setup, submission, testing tracks, and release verification.

## 9. Build quality into the first feature

Start with one small, team-agreed change. Define its expected behavior before writing or generating code.

| Check | Example question |
| :--- | :--- |
| **Business logic** | Does the rule return the expected result for valid, empty, and invalid inputs? |
| **State transitions** | Does loading become content, empty, or a recoverable error correctly? |
| **Cancellation and accounts** | Can an older request or a previous account overwrite current state? |
| **Data integration** | Are mapping, cache behavior, and failure handling verified? |
| **UI and accessibility** | Can a user complete the journey with the required input and accessibility modes? |
| **Telemetry privacy** | Are diagnostic events useful without exposing credentials or sensitive data? |

The documentation's required coverage target is **100% within the explicitly defined coverage scope**. The Testing & Quality section defines applicable metrics and enforcement. Do not silently exclude production files or treat a coverage percentage as evidence that all behavior is correct.

AI-generated tests remain drafts until reviewed and executed. Provide only approved context; never include real credentials in prompts. The agreed CI workflow executes committed, reviewed tests without calling an LLM to generate them during the run.

## 10. Check your understanding

Before continuing, make sure you can explain:

- [ ] Why a request can finish after its screen or account is no longer current.
- [ ] Which state belongs to a screen, a shared owner, or persistent storage.
- [ ] Why connectivity checks and circuit breakers do not guarantee successful operations.
- [ ] Why a mobile app cannot safely hold a privileged backend secret.
- [ ] Why Android and iOS need separate verification even with shared React Native code.
- [ ] Why a passed local build is different from a verified release.
- [ ] Which tests and diagnostic signals your first change needs.

**Next: [Machine Setup — macOS Apple Silicon](/mobile/01-onboarding/machine-setup-guide/)** — prepare access and install the tools required by your assigned application.




